---
/**
 * A list of concept links in up to two columns, collapsing to one when its container is
 * narrower than 26rem.
 *
 * ── WHAT THIS IS, AND WHY IT IS NOT CALLED `TopicGrid` ──────────────────────
 *
 * Promoted 2026-09-13 from three copies — `index.astro`, `search.astro` and
 * `SearchResults.astro` — well past this project's "a second consumer triggers promotion"
 * rule, and deferred that long only so search work would not edit the shipped Home page.
 *
 * Extracting it as a TOPIC grid turned out not to work. `index.astro` shares ONE rule
 * block between `.topic-grid` and `.genre-list`, so pulling topics out on their own would
 * have left the 26rem threshold in two places again — which is the exact drift the
 * extraction exists to stop. Every consumer lists SKOS concepts from one of the two
 * vocabularies: Selected Topics and Genres on Home, Common Topics on search and the 404.
 * Hence `ConceptList`, named for what it holds rather than for the first caller.
 *
 * ── IT OWNS ITS CONTAINER, WHICH IS THE POINT ───────────────────────────────
 *
 * A container query resolves against the nearest ANCESTOR container — an element cannot
 * query itself — so the three copies each set `container-type: inline-size` on whatever
 * happened to wrap the list: `.topics-col`, `.genres-col`, `.search-topics`. That made the
 * threshold a contract the caller had to remember, which is half of why it drifted.
 *
 * So this renders its own containing wrapper. The wrapper is block-level and fills the
 * caller's column, so its inline size is identical to what those three elements measured,
 * and every consumer's rendering is unchanged — verified at 375 and 1024 against the
 * built page before the extraction.
 *
 * ── THE HEADING AND THE LANDMARK STAY AT THE CALL SITE ──────────────────────
 *
 * Deliberately not absorbed. The three consumers label their lists differently, wire
 * `aria-labelledby` to different ids, and `SearchResults` additionally needs `hidden` and
 * a `data-` hook on its `<nav>`. Whether a list earns a `<nav>` landmark at all is a
 * semantics decision, which belongs with the page that knows what the list is for.
 *
 * ── THE MARGIN IS ON THE WRAPPER, NOT THE LIST ──────────────────────────────
 *
 * `container-type` implies `contain: layout`, which stops margins escaping the wrapper —
 * so a margin on the list would be trapped inside it and ADD to any bottom margin on the
 * heading above rather than collapsing with it. It happens not to matter today (`h2` is
 * `margin-bottom: 0`, measured), but putting it on the wrapper is the arrangement that
 * stays correct if that ever changes.
 */
interface Props {
  /** Omit entirely to render the empty shell for a script to fill — see SearchResults. */
  items?: Array<{label: string; href: string}>
  class?: string
  /* Forwarded to the <ul>, which is what carries `data-search-topic-list` and anything
     else a caller hangs off the list itself. Same contract as <Chip>. */
  [key: string]: unknown
}

const {items = [], class: className, ...rest}: Props = Astro.props
---

<div class="concept-list-box">
  {/* `role="list"` because `list-style: none` drops list semantics in Safari. */}
  <ul class:list={['concept-list', className]} role="list" {...rest}>
    {
      items.map((item) => (
        <li>
          <a href={item.href}>{item.label}</a>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>
</div>

<style>
  .concept-list-box {
    container-type: inline-size;
    margin-top: var(--rhythm-heading-close);
  }

  /*
   * ── ROW-MAJOR, WHICH IS WHAT MAKES THE ALPHABET READ CORRECTLY ────────────
   *
   * Two columns filled LEFT TO RIGHT THEN DOWN, so an a–z runs across each row rather
   * than down the first column. Andy's call (2026-09-03), citing the footer's site index.
   *
   * NOTHING HERE SETS `grid-auto-flow`, and that is the point: row-major IS the default,
   * so the DOM order is the reading order and the alphabet is already correct in the
   * markup. A screen reader and a sighted reader get the same sequence — the property the
   * Insights masonry was rebuilt to recover.
   *
   * `--grid-gutter-content` is the column gap and already carries the board's two values:
   * DESIGN.md reserves it for gaps between real content columns and defines it "larger on
   * mobile, not smaller" — 32 stacked, 24 at md. The boards draw exactly that, 156 + 32 +
   * 156 at 375 and 274 + 24 + 274 at 1440. Neither number is restated here.
   *
   * base.css keeps the UA's list indent for prose; a nav is not prose, hence `padding: 0`.
   *
   * One column is the base case, per tokens.css's mobile-first rule: an engine that never
   * evaluates the query below must be left with the narrow layout.
   */
  .concept-list {
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;

    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    row-gap: var(--rhythm-heading-close);
    column-gap: var(--grid-gutter-content);
  }

  /*
   * ── A SIZE CONTAINER QUERY, WHICH IS THE MINOR-BREAKPOINT MODEL ───────────
   *
   * tokens.css: a MINOR breakpoint is "a single component adjusting to the space IT has",
   * and it says to prefer a real size container query. The question here is genuinely
   * local — does THIS list's column have room for two of its longest label?
   *
   * ── 26rem IS MEASURED, AND IT IS ALSO VERY NEARLY THE ONLY VALUE ──────────
   *
   * The longest labels are "Information Architecture" (198px at the desktop step) and
   * "Conference Themes" (158px). Two columns plus the gutter therefore need about 416 and
   * 348 of container. The link step is fluid, so the labels are ~5% narrower where this
   * threshold bites, which the measurement accounts for:
   *
   *     vw    container   gap   topic col   longest topic
   *     375     343        32     155.5         187      wraps  -> one column
   *     448     416        32     192           188      fits   -> two columns
   *     768     419        24     197.5         192      fits
   *    1440     571        24     273.5         198      fits
   *
   * The window is narrow and worth naming, because the obvious larger number breaks
   * something invisible: the 7-column TRACK at 768 is 419px, which is NARROWER than the
   * stacked column at a 475px viewport (443). Any threshold above 419 would quietly
   * collapse the Topics list to one column at 768 as well. So 26rem is close to the
   * ceiling, not a round number picked for looks — and Andy's 475px viewport figure is not
   * reachable this way without that side effect. It switches at about 448px instead, which
   * is where the measurement says the wrapping actually stops.
   *
   * ── IT ALSO RETIRES THE GENRES SPECIAL CASE ───────────────────────────────
   *
   * The genre list used to carry an explicit md rule dropping it to one column, with a
   * comment explaining why it "inverts". That was a hand-written statement of something
   * the container derives: its md track is 229px and its lg track 316px, both under 26rem,
   * so one column falls out on its own. The inversion was never a rule, only a consequence
   * of the column being narrow, and it reads much better as one.
   *
   * The same derivation covers the search rail, which is 316 at lg and 229 at md — under
   * the threshold at every width it ever has, so it is one column there and two only below
   * md, where it is full width.
   */
  @container (inline-size >= 26rem) {
    .concept-list {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
    }
  }
</style>
